.TH LINUXCTL 1 "September 2026" "linux-mcp-daemon" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linuxctl \- Command line interface for the Linux MCP Daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fI<verb> <group> [target-keyword] [args]\fR
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIdescribe <group> [target-keyword] <name>\fR
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIexplain <group>\fR
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIget mcp-api <tools|resources|prompts|info>\fR
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fItool <group>/<command>\fR [\fI\-\-flag val ...\fR]
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIresource <uri>\fR
.br
.B linuxctl
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fI<verb> mcpd user <username>\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linuxctl
is the client utility for interacting with the Linux Model Context Protocol (MCP) Daemon (mcpd). It allows users and AI agents to send commands to the daemon running in the background.
.PP
Instead of one flat command per tool, \fBlinuxctl\fR speaks a small, uniform verb/group grammar: every tool, resource, and resource template mcpd exposes is reachable through the same handful of words, discovered dynamically from the daemon's live schema on every invocation. Adding a new tool server-side makes it usable via \fBlinuxctl\fR immediately, with no client changes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-server \ \fIURL\fR
The URL of the mcpd server to connect to. Defaults to MCP_SERVER, else https://localhost:9091.
.TP
.BR \-token \ \fITOKEN\fR
Bearer token for authentication. If not provided via this flag, the client will look for the \fBMCP_TOKEN\fR environment variable. Not needed for the local-only \fBmcpd\fR admin group.
.TP
.BR \-config\-path \ \fIDIR\fR
Local path to the daemon's \fBconfigs/\fR directory. Only used by the local-only \fBmcpd\fR admin group. Defaults to \fB./configs\fR.
.SH THE GRAMMAR
.PP
.B linuxctl <verb> <group> [target-keyword] [name/args]
.PP
\fB<group>\fR is always present, always the tool's real \fBtools_group\fR (\fBfiles\fR, \fBdisks\fR, \fBprocesses\fR, \fBnetwork\fR, \fBdevices\fR, \fBkernel\fR, \fBlogs\fR, \fBsystem\fR, \fBusers\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBmemory\fR, \fBauth\fR). There is no top-level \fBservices\fR group \- \fBservices/list\fR and \fBservices/manage\fR live under \fBsystem\fR, reached as \fBsystem services\fR.
.PP
\fB<verb>\fR is one of exactly two universal words for reads-and-detail, plus a tool's own action word for mutations:
.TP
.B get
The sole read verb, one result or many, no separate \fBlist\fR \- mirrors \fBkubectl\fR exactly (\fBkubectl get pods\fR vs \fBkubectl get pod nginx\fR, same verb, disambiguated by whether a name is given).
.TP
.B describe
Rich, aggregated single-object detail, backed by resource templates. Not redundant with \fBget <group> <name>\fR: \fBget\fR returns the same lightweight format the many-result case uses, just filtered; \fBdescribe\fR combines several reads into one report and deliberately excludes secret-shaped data (e.g. a process's \fBenviron\fR) by default.
.TP
.B mutation words
\fBcreate\fR, \fBupdate\fR, \fBdelete\fR, \fBrestart\fR, \fBstart\fR, \fBstop\fR, \fBenable\fR, \fBdisable\fR keep their own verb directly. The last five are enum values read from a tool's own schema, not hardcoded in the client.
.PP
\fB[target-keyword]\fR disambiguates which operation within the group, only needed when the group has more than one candidate under that verb. \fBtop\fR is one such keyword (not a verb) \- \fBget processes top\fR calls the \fBprocesses/top\fR tool: a \fBtop \-b \-n 1\fR style snapshot with the load/tasks/CPU/memory header and all of top's columns.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ping
Pings the mcpd daemon to verify connectivity and authentication.
.TP
.B resource <uri>
Reads the exact contents of an MCP resource directly by URI. URIs use \fBscheme://path\fR syntax, e.g. \fBos://uname\fR, \fBfile:///etc/hosts\fR.
.TP
.B explain <group>
Lists every tool, resource, and template reachable in a group, with the exact verb/keyword for each \- generated dynamically from the live schema.
.TP
.B get mcp-api <tools|resources|prompts|info>
A reserved meta-group for the MCP protocol's own top-level catalog concepts, not a real \fBtools_group\fR. \fBinfo\fR prints the raw \fBinitialize\fR response (protocol version and declared capabilities); \fBprompts\fR calls the real \fBprompts/list\fR method and reports plainly that this daemon doesn't implement it, rather than pretending the catalog is empty.
.TP
.B <verb> <group> [target-keyword] [args]
The main grammar described above. Arguments can be positional or explicit flags. \fB\-\-output table\fR, \fBwide\fR, \fByaml\fR, or \fBjson\fR formats the result, and is also forwarded as the underlying tool's own \fBoutput_format\fR argument where it has one.
.TP
.B tool <group>/<command> [\-\-flag val ...]
Calls a tool directly by its literal name, symmetric with \fBresource <uri>\fR. Also the pre-redesign flat-syntax escape hatch for anything the verb resolver doesn't yet cover.
.TP
.B <verb> mcpd user <username>
User/token administration: create, delete, update (rotate token), list, or describe an mcpd bearer-token user. Edits \fBconfigs/users.yaml\fR and \fBconfigs/mcp-sudo.yaml\fR directly on disk (path set by \fB\-\-config\-path\fR, default \fB./configs\fR); there is no MCP tool that edits them. A newly created user's token is printed once, to the terminal only, and is never stored in plaintext - only its salted hash is written to \fBusers.yaml\fR. \fB\-\-grant\fR \fITOOL,...\fR lets a new user run those tools as root. After a change, asks the running daemon to re-read its files (\fBdaemon/reload-config\fR, with \fBMCP_SERVER\fR and \fBMCP_TOKEN\fR); \fB\-\-no\-reload\fR skips that.
.TP
.B edit mcpd config [sudo|users|daemon]
Edits one config file like \fBvisudo\fR(8): a copy is opened in \fB$VISUAL\fR or \fB$EDITOR\fR and, on save, checked with the same strict parser mcpd uses; only a valid file replaces the original, and the daemon is then reloaded. An invalid edit can be edited again or discarded.
.TP
.B reload daemon
Makes the running mcpd re-read daemon.yaml, users.yaml and mcp-sudo.yaml without a restart (\fBdaemon/reload-config\fR; needs that tool granted in mcp-sudo.yaml). Prints what changed; an invalid file leaves the running config untouched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MCP_TOKEN
The bearer token used for authenticating with the daemon. Can be used instead of the \fB\-token\fR flag.
.TP
.B MCP_SERVER
The mcpd URL, instead of \fB\-server\fR (default https://localhost:9091).
.TP
.B MCP_TLS_FINGERPRINT
Trust the server whose certificate has this SHA-256 fingerprint (\fBsha256:\fIhex\fR), e.g. mcpd's self-signed one; \fBlinuxctl describe mcpd tls\fR shows it on the daemon's host.
.TP
.B MCP_CA_CERT
Trust this PEM certificate file for the server.
.TP
.B MCP_TLS_INSECURE
Set to 1 to skip certificate verification, like \fBcurl \-k\fR (testing only); or pass \fB\-tls\-insecure\fR / \fB\-k\fR. Any certificate is then accepted, a forged one too, and the token is sent to it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Ping the daemon using the default server and a specific token:
.IP
.B linuxctl \-token "my-test-token-123" ping
.PP
Ping the daemon using an environment variable:
.IP
.B export MCP_TOKEN="my-test-token-123"
.br
.B linuxctl ping
.PP
List all available resources:
.IP
.B linuxctl get mcp-api resources
.PP
Read the system uname as a resource:
.IP
.B linuxctl resource os://uname
.PP
Read connected USB devices as a formatted table:
.IP
.B linuxctl get devices usb --output table
.PP
List a directory (explicit \fBlist\fR keyword \- see the man text for why files needs it):
.IP
.B linuxctl get files list /var/log --privileged true
.PP
Read a single file's contents (bare \- files' only other read candidate):
.IP
.B linuxctl get files /etc/hosts
.PP
Native partition geometry, parsed from /sys/class/block (no fdisk dependency):
.IP
.B linuxctl get disks partitions vda --output json
.nf
[
  {
    "device": "vda1",
    "parent_disk": "vda",
    "number": 1,
    "start_sector": 2048,
    "size_sectors": 124997632,
    "size_bytes": 63998787584
  }
]
.fi
.PP
Read, then write, a kernel parameter (same tool, sysctl equivalent):
.IP
.B linuxctl get kernel sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward
.nf
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
.fi
.IP
.B linuxctl update kernel sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward 1 --privileged true
.PP
Query the systemd journal for the current boot only (requires \fB\-\-privileged true\fR in containerized deployments, since journalctl only exists on the host):
.IP
.B linuxctl get logs journal --unit kubelet.service --boot true --privileged true
.PP
One result vs many, same underlying tool:
.IP
.B linuxctl get processes --sort_by mem --limit 5
.br
.B linuxctl get processes 1234
.PP
Aggregated single-object detail (combines several reads, excludes environ):
.IP
.B linuxctl describe processes 1234
.PP
Create a new mcpd user (local-only, prints the token once):
.IP
.B linuxctl create mcpd user alice
.nf
Created mcpd user "alice".

Token (shown once - not stored in plaintext anywhere, save it now):
  863953e1c3a24cee64c4c2306af990da5fd9041bb8c9db5f0d48d478df5d860d
.fi
